00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:43.010
Um, so with this, let's say Senator Sanders,
um, somehow is able to actually win the nomination

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.940
and does end up wanting the presidential race.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:48.940
What do you think the odds are that you will
actually get a Medicare for all bill pass?

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:54.649
Because it seems like even those who do support
Medicare for all have some watered down version

00:00:54.649 --> 00:00:55.649
of it.

00:00:55.649 --> 00:01:00.520
Like, so how many do you think would actually
vote for it if it were to be put put on the

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:02.079
bill for everyone to vote on?

00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:06.461
This is a very important question, which is
that even within the democratic party, there

00:01:06.461 --> 00:01:11.159
is not, um, sort of unanimous consent around
number one.

00:01:11.159 --> 00:01:13.820
Not everybody in the democratic party supports
Medicare for all.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:18.400
And even those that support something that
they call Medicare for all, they don't necessarily

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.790
support it in the exact way in formulation
that Bernie Sanders does.

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:26.710
But I don't think that this is particularly
relevant because what we've seen is that the

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:34.320
more, um, uh, progressive position that you
start the negotiation from, the better off

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:35.360
we will end up.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:41.180
So Bernie Sanders can't control whether Democrats
take control of the Senate in 2020 or 2022.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:46.810
Bernie Sanders can't, at least not directly
control, uh, what the majority of the democratic

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:50.560
majority is in the house or whether they maintain
one after the midterm.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.790
All he can do is say, here's my starting point.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:59.560
And then he will negotiate with whoever is
there for the most progressive possible plan.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:03.700
So I think you're bringing up a perfectly
reasonable question, which is, is it guaranteed

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.829
that a president Bernie gets Medicare for
all?

00:02:05.829 --> 00:02:07.590
The answer is definitely not.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:08.590
Definitely not.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:09.590
And this is just a reality.

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:13.190
I mean, it's not a pro or anti Bernie bias
thing, it's just, there's no guarantee that

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:15.250
any president gets done what they want to
do.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:19.090
The more audacious it is, the more difficult
it will be to do.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:24.360
But we still want to be starting from the
best possible place.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.380
Right.

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:28.980
