00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:41.000
Someone brings up Canada, UK off from the,
the number one, uh, pivot from, from the right

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.000
six of you.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.950
Well, but there, you know, population is significantly
less than I was.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:45.950
That's right.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:47.739
They're able to, you know, have a system like
that.

00:02:47.739 --> 00:02:52.860
So run on how to care for all at a state level
instead of a national level would actually

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:53.920
be any better.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.540
Um, just be [inaudible] it seems like it would
be a little easier to manage, or at least

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:59.660
it's the way I see it right now.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:02.870
So I dealt with this earlier this week, so
I won't, I won't do the full explanation,

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:04.300
but, so two things on that.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:08.590
Number one, I disagree with the premise that
if you have more people, it's more difficult

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:15.170
to do because more people actually means that
the, the risk pool is larger and no one group

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:21.280
within that population is going to throw off
your actuarial predictions, um, nearly as

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.280
much.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:23.720
So I reject that on principle.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:28.810
That being said, I have no problem with Medicare
for all or other single payer systems starting

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:29.810
at the state level.

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:33.490
I think that from a pragmatic perspective,
you might much more quickly be able to get

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:34.490
something done.

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:38.350
So I'm not opposed to it in any way, but the
population argument is a tired one.

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:39.350
[inaudible]

